{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-277985",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The Law of Sines states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡X}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡Z}{z}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\nx=14,\\,m∠Z={40}^{∘},\\,z=10,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡X}{14} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}}{10} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡X & =14\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}}{10} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{14\\cdot 0.643}{10} \\\\  & ≈0.900,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠X & ≈\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.900)≈{64.158}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut this is less than ${90}^{∘}.$ To get the true value of $X,$ we subtract our answer from ${180}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\nm∠X≈{180}^{∘}-{64.158}^{∘}≈{116}^{∘},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest degree.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
